Linear Spring System Structural Analysis

Project Overview
This project focuses on the modeling, execution, and verification of a discrete linear spring-mass system. The primary goal is to evaluate the displacements and internal forces of interconnected spring elements under structural gravitational loading conditions.

Engineering Setup and System Configuration

Software Environment: Abaqus Student Edition

Element Formulations Used: Linear Spring elements (SpringA) and point Mass elements (MASS).

Material Properties: Defined directly via elemental spring stiffness constants (k1 = 1000 N/mm, k2 = 2000 N/mm, k3 = 3000 N/mm) and nodal mass definitions (10 kg).

Boundary Conditions: Rigid displacement and rotation constraints applied to specific node sets to simulate fixed foundation conditions.

Loading Profile: Gravitational load step applied uniformly to the system mass distributions.

Analysis Strategy and Implementation
Unlike continuous solid geometries, this model uses a discrete structural approach where individual nodal coordinates are explicitly mapped to manage load transfer. The solution tracking is verified directly from the pure text input file (.inp), which dictates:

Assembly mapping across consecutive node arrays.

Boundary constraints isolating movement to a single axis of translation.

Static equilibrium solver parameters tracking displacement outputs (U) and element forces.

Key Engineering Takeaways

Discrete Element Proficiency: Demonstrates core competency in building stylized spring, dashpot, and localized mass networks in Abaqus without relying on complex CAD geometries.

Input File Verification: Showcases the ability to interpret and construct clean Abaqus Input Files (.inp), proving a fundamental understanding of how finite element solvers parse node sets, element loops, and load step properties behind the graphical user interface.
